Finding: {"title":"Legacy QuickBooks OAuth flow is gated by a single global env var with no per-tenant scoping, and defaults enabled in test env","severity":"LOW","area":"business-rules / configuration-risk","files":["src/server/routes/routes-quickbooks.ts"],"detail":"`legacyUserScopedQuickBooksEnabled()` (src/server/routes/routes-quickbooks.ts:117-123) gates the entire legacy OAuth/callback/sync/disconnect flow behind one process-wide `ENABLE_LEGACY_USER_SCOPED_QB_SYNC` env var for all schools and users simultaneously; there is no per-school migration flag, and the flag defaults true in `NODE_ENV === 'test'`.","impact":"Low likelihood but non-trivial blast radius: an accidental flip of this single flag in production re-opens `/quickbooks/auth-url`, `/quickbooks/callback`, `/quickbooks/sync/*` for all tenants at once, potentially creating `userIntegrations` rows that diverge from the intended `qb_connections`/`accounting_provider_connections` system of record, and re-exposes the invoiceUrl-discard and query-escaping bugs above.","recommendation":"Once the migration off the legacy path is confirmed complete, remove the legacy routes/flag entirely rather than leaving a global kill-switch that can silently reactivate a superseded subsystem.","confidence":"LOW"}
